  "verdict": "CONFIRMED with a nuance. All four sub-claims are reproduced from raw summaries: (1) OOD paired error is 3.395-3.730x in-domain on all 3 graphs (within the claimed 3.4-3.7 band, low-degree actually 3.395-3.398 which is just below the stated 3.4 lower bound); (2) all pairs worse (fraction=1.0, Wilcoxon stat = max W1 = 500500); (3) p ~ 1.66e-165 < alpha 0.001 (this is the NORMAL-APPROX paired-Wilcoxon p, not exact); (4) high-degree graph amplifies most (3.730). Refutation attempts all failed to refute the core claim. The high-degree 'amplification' is REAL but MILD: its ratio lift is driven by a LOWER in-domain baseline (0.4838 vs 0.5235/0.5262), and the raw out-domain means differ by only 0.026 pp between high- and low-degree graphs.",
  "counter_evidence": "1) The exact one-sided Wilcoxon p = 1/C(2000,1000) ~ 1e-600 underflows float64; the reported 1.66e-165 is a NORMAL-APPROXIMATION value (scipy method='normal', continuity correction) at the max rank-sum, not the exact combinatorial p. Both are < 0.001, so the significance claim stands, but the quoted 1.66e-165 is an approximation. 2) The '100% of pairs worse' claim rests on stored fraction=1.0 and W1=max; the raw per-pair arrays are NOT in the portable JSON (only SHA256 hashes), so this is an internal-consistency check, not a direct per-pair re-derivation. 3) High-degree amplification is mild (ratio 3.730 vs 3.395-3.398, a 0.33 pp absolute / ~9.9% relative lift) and largely attributable to a lower in-domain baseline rather than a genuinely different OOD failure. 4) Low-degree ratio 3.395-3.398 sits just below the claimed '3.4' lower bound; the 3.4-3.7 band is a loose rounding of 3.395-3.730."
